callee-shape-measured-0.28pct		354	tooling	MEASURED at last, instead of guessed. The single-unresolved-call shape was swept properly: all 354 unlanded structural-tier candidates run through explain_mismatch, which is free and spends no attempt. 41 of 354 (11.6 percent) showed the surface signature - classification 'unresolved REL32 call' together with target-size equal to compiled-size. But that signature is mostly a FALSE POSITIVE. Running decode_calls over those 41 resolved the REL32 target to a real already-matched function in only 8 cases (about 20 percent); the other 80 percent decoded to garbage addresses, meaning the candidate's PLACEMENT ITSELF is wrong rather than a pin merely being absent. Of the 8 genuine cases, pinning and re-checking left exactly ONE at exact match; the other 7 had register-allocation, stack-offset or encoding differences EARLIER in the body. Net yield: 1 landed row per 354 candidates swept, 0.28 percent - or 12.5 percent among candidates whose callee genuinely resolved. ROOT CAUSE of the illusion, and the reusable lesson: explain_mismatch's classify() reports 'unresolved REL32 call' whenever ANY unresolved call exists, regardless of whether other differences also exist and regardless of their position, so the classification systematically hides earlier non-call diffs. Never trust that classification as meaning call-only. CORRECTED PROCEDURE for any future pass: run decode_calls FIRST to reject the ~80 percent garbage-placement majority, then pin, then re-run explain_mismatch and require a literal exact match before believing the candidate was ever call-only. Do not schedule this as a primary lane; the yield does not justify it
structural-pool-placement-quality		584	tooling	POOL QUALITY finding that reframes the structural tier. Roughly 80 percent of the structural-tier candidates that reach decode_calls decode to garbage addresses - 'no known function here, the row's target_rva or the placement is probably wrong'. So a large majority of this pool is not blocked on source reconstruction at all; it is blocked on the row's ADDRESS being wrong. That is why aligned_pct in this tier tops out around 90 and why so many candidates diverge from byte 0 with an entirely different register-argument convention (testMinimumRequirements is the clearest example - it looks mismatched or wrong-signature rather than carrying a bounded diff). Practical consequence: for this tier, VALIDATE THE PLACEMENT BEFORE INVESTING. locate.py's masked byte-scan and decode_calls are both cheap and both answer that question. Distribution over 16 candidates examined in detail from the ranked head (90 percent down to 48 percent aligned): 1 free exact match, 1 deep structural difference needing full source reconstruction, 2 named scheduling residuals (RankPoints ctor has an SEH frame our body lacks with no destructible locals to explain it; ParabolicEase::operator() is pure FPU register-reuse-versus-reload), 1 wrong-signature, 2 that classified as unresolved-REL32 but whose callee was ALREADY matched so the real difference was direct-call versus IAT-indirect-call codegen, and the rest only skimmed. Worth returning to only with placement validation up front
