adjacency-placement-is-the-method	0x0091CD30	4	tooling	THE GENERALISABLE METHOD hiding behind the four engine-ref accessors, and the reason they landed when the byte-scan approach cannot work for bodies of their size. They were not placed by a byte scan at all - at 4 bytes they fall far below locate.py's 8-byte needle floor. They were placeable because of ADJACENCY: each sat in an address gap bracketed on both sides by ALREADY-MATCHED rows, 16-byte aligned, leaving exactly one consistent assignment. That is a placement signal completely independent of byte entropy, and it is the only one that can work for sub-8-byte bodies. The WW3D2 neighbourhood was checked exhaustively for further instances of the pattern and has NONE left - dx8vertexbuffer.cpp and dx8indexbuffer.cpp are confirmed fully harvested and no sibling file has an equivalent gap - which is why that lane returned nothing. But the check was only ever run against a handful of files. The obvious next step is a tool that enumerates, binary-wide, every address gap between consecutive matched rows and cross-references the gap size and alignment against ledger-absent definitions of a compatible compiled size. That would make the sub-8-byte population reachable for the first time. Worth building before running another pass at this category
